.\" ===================================================================
.SH GETTING STARTED
.SS Web Portal
Login or create an account at the web portal.
The portal serves static HTML pages and communicates with the game server
through a REST API.
.SS Lobby
The lobby displays open game rooms.
A player can create a new room (which selects a universe module) or join
an existing room by taking seat A or seat B.
.SS Starting a Game
When both seats are filled, either player may start the game.
In single-player mode, an AI user occupies the second seat automatically.
.SS Module Selection
The game host selects the universe module at room creation time.
A \fBModule\fR defines a complete game universe: map topology, star systems,
planets, species, resources, anomalies, and facilities.
The game engine is module-agnostic; only content varies between modules.
.PP
The default module is \fBKepler's Horizon\fR: 28 star systems across
Alpha and Beta territories, with a Contested Reach between them.
.\" ===================================================================
.SH MAP COMPONENTS
The game map is a hex grid overlaid with named star systems and warpline
connections.
Not every hex contains a star.
.TP
.B Star Hex
A map hex containing a star system (large or small).
Star hexes are the nodes of the warpline graph.
Ships must stop at enemy-occupied star hexes.
.TP
.B Base Star Hex
A star hex containing a large star.
There are six base stars total, three per side (Alpha and Beta).
Occupying enemy base stars earns victory points.
.TP
.B Space Hex
A hex with no star and no warpline.
Ships may pass through freely.
.TP
.B Warp Line
A bi-directional edge connecting two star hexes.
Traversing a warpline costs 1 PD regardless of distance.
Warplines pass through intermediate hexes but those hexes are treated
as space hexes for movement purposes.
.\" ===================================================================
.SH SHIP TYPES AND ATTRIBUTES
.SS "Ship Types"
.TP
.B WarpShip (W1\-W9)
Capital ships capable of interstellar travel via warplines.
WarpShips carry a Warp Generator (5 CR, mandatory) and may equip
SystemShip Racks to carry escort vessels.
Hull codes are W followed by a single digit: W1, W2, ... W9.
A fleet may contain up to 9 WarpShips.
.TP
.B SystemShip (S01\-S99)
Escort vessels that operate within a single star hex.
SystemShips have no Warp Generator and cannot traverse warplines.
They must be carried by WarpShips in SystemShip Racks.
Hull codes are S followed by one or two digits: S1, S11, S25, etc.
.SS "Ship Attributes"
Every ship has the following combat-relevant attributes, each costing
Credit Points (CR) at construction time:
.TS
tab(|);
l l l l.
Attribute|Code|Cost|Description
_
Power/Drive|PD|1 CR/unit|Engine strength; powers movement and combat
Warp Generator|WG|5 CR|WarpShips only (automatic, mandatory)
Phasic|P|1 CR/unit|Energy weapon; damage = allocation + tech level
Shield|S|1 CR/unit|Energy shield; absorbs = allocation + tech level
Launcher|L|1 CR/launcher|Launches 1 torpedo per combat round
Torpedoes|T|1 CR/3|Projectile weapons; damage = 2 + tech level
SystemShip Hangar|H|1 CR/hangar|Carries 1 SystemShip per hangar (WarpShips only)
.TE
.PP
Additionally, ships may be equipped with economic hardware:
.TS
tab(|);
l l l l.
Equipment|Code|Cost|Purpose
_
Long Range Scanner|LRS|50 CR|Required for \fBextract scan\fR and \fBsurvey\fR
.TE
.PP
Equipment does not affect combat.
A ship is destroyed only when all combat attributes (PD, P, S, L, T, H)
are reduced to zero.
The Warp Generator never takes damage.
.SS "Technology Levels"
Ships retain their original tech level permanently.
Tech level is determined by the game round at construction time:
.TS
tab(|);
l l.
Rounds|Tech Level
_
1\-4|Level 0
5\-8|Level 1
9\-12|Level 2
13\-16|Level 3
17+|+1 per 4 rounds
.TE
.PP
Tech level adds to:
.IP \(bu 2
Phasic damage (when hitting)
.IP \(bu 2
Torpedo damage (base 2 + tech level)
.IP \(bu 2
Shield absorption (when powered)
.PP
A Tech Level 2 ship with Phasic 3 allocated to phasics deals 5 damage on a hit.
A Tech Level 2 ship with Shield 2 allocated absorbs 4 hits.
.\" ===================================================================
.SH GAME SEQUENCE
Each player-turn consists of the following phases, in order.
The command \fBnext\fR (alias \fBn\fR) advances to the next phase.
The command \fBdone\fR (alias \fBZZ\fR) ends the current player's turn
and passes initiative to the opponent.
.PP
.RS
.IP "1." 4
\fBCount Victory Points.\fR
At the start of each player-turn, the system awards 1 VP for each
enemy base star hex occupied by your ships.
.IP "2." 4
\fBBuild Ships.\fR
Receive Stellar Credits (CR) and spend Credit Points (CR) to construct
new ships, repair damaged ships, resupply torpedoes, and manage equipment.
.IP "3." 4
\fBMovement.\fR
Move WarpShips along hex paths and warplines.
Pick up and drop off SystemShips.
Deploy newly built ships to your base star hexes.
.IP "4." 4
\fBResolve Combat.\fR
At every star hex where opposing ships coexist, combat is triggered.
Players issue simultaneous orders, resolve fire, assign damage,
and handle retreats.
.IP "5." 4
\fBSystemShip Pick and Drop.\fR
Free rearrangement of SystemShips at star hexes.
No PD cost.
Active player only.
.IP "6." 4
\fBEnd of Turn.\fR
Finalize the turn.
Market prices update after both players complete a round.
.RE
.\" ===================================================================
.SH COMMANDS
Commands are case-insensitive.
Short aliases are shown after commas.
All commands are entered in the console text field on the game page.
.SS "Session Commands"
.TP
.B save \fIname\fR
Save the current game state under \fIname\fR.
The name must match the lexer pattern
.BR [a\-zA\-Z][\-a\-zA\-Z0\-9]* :
letters, digits, and dashes only.
No underscores, dots, or spaces.
Without arguments, shows usage.
.TP
.B load \fIname\fR
Propose loading a previously saved game.
This initiates a two-factor confirmation: the other player must
type \fBaccept \fIname\fR\fR to confirm.
Without arguments, lists available saves.
.TP
.B accept \fIname\fR
Accept a pending load request from the other player.
The game state is restored from the saved snapshot.
.TP
.B reject \fIname\fR
Reject a pending load request.
.TP
.B delete \fIname\fR
Delete a saved game permanently.
.TP
.B quit
Exit the current game.
The game is auto-saved as \fBautosaved-YYYY-MM-DD-HH-MM\fR.
Combat state is nullified (ships remain in position; combat will
re-trigger on reload).
The other player receives a notification and is redirected to the lobby.
The AI thread's game context is zeroed so it naturally ceases
operating on this game.
.TP
.B clear\fR, \fBcls
Clear the console display.
.SS "Turn Management"
.TP
.B next\fR, \fBn
Advance to the next game phase.
Blocked if there are pending retreats or unresolved damage.
.TP
.B done\fR, \fBZZ
Complete the current player's turn and pass initiative to the opponent.
Blocked if there are pending retreats.
.SS "Information Commands"
.TP
.B score
Display victory points and credits for both players, plus current
round and tech level and display current game state: whose turn, phase, round, credits, tech level.
.TP
.B fleet
Display a manifest of your entire fleet: every ship with its hull code,
name, location, and current attribute values (PD, P, S, L, T, H, equipment).
.TP
.B cargo \fIship\fR
Display the cargo manifest for \fIship\fR: quantities of each resource
type in the hold, cargo capacity, and torpedo storage.
.TP
.B hex \fIlocation\fR
Show information about hex \fIlocation\fR: which ships occupy it,
which star system (if any), active hex events, and warpline connections.
.TP
.B system \fIname\fR\fR, \fBsy \fIname\fR
Show information about star system \fIname\fR.
Subcommands:
.RS
.TP
.B system \fIname\fR anomalies
List anomalies at the system.
.TP
.B system \fIname\fR facilities
List facilities at the system.
.TP
.B system \fIname\fR resources
List extractable resources at the system.
.TP
.B system \fIname\fR planets
List planets and moons in the system.
.RE
.TP
.B survey \fIname\fR\fR, \fBsv \fIname\fR
Survey a star system to increase your knowledge level (see MILIEU CODEX).
Requires a ship with LRS at the target system.
Each survey operation upgrades knowledge by one level.
Without arguments, shows survey status.
.TP
.B crt
Display the Combat Results Table.
.TP
.B help \fItopic\fR\fR, \fB? \fItopic\fR
Show help for a command or topic.
Without arguments, lists all available topics.
Topics: general, build, move, combat, economy, info, turn, ship, crt, torpedoes.
.TP
.B recap
Display a summary of recent game events.
.SS "Build Commands"
.TP
.B build\fR, \fBbb
Show current build state: active drafts and available CR.
.TP
.B build new \fIclass\fR \fIname\fR\fR, \fBbn \fIclass\fR \fIname\fR
Create a new ship draft.
\fIclass\fR is W (WarpShip) or S (SystemShip), optionally followed by
1\-2 digits for a specific hull number (e.g., W4, S11).
If no number is given, the game auto-assigns the next available hull code.
\fIname\fR is the ship designation (e.g., Falcon, Avenger).
.RS
.PP
\fBExamples:\fR
.nf
> BN W FALCON
> BN S11 AVENGER
> BUILD NEW W4 DESTROYER
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B build drafts\fR, \fBbd
List all pending build drafts.
.TP
.B build drafts \fIship\fR\fR, \fBbd \fIship\fR
Show detailed attributes of a specific draft (by code or name).
.TP
.B build set \fItarget\fR \fIattributes\fR\fR, \fBbs \fItarget\fR \fIattributes\fR
Set attributes on a draft ship.
\fItarget\fR is ship code or name.
Attribute syntax:
.RS
.TP
.BR PD= N " or " D= N
Power/Drive (1 CR per unit)
.TP
.BR P= N
Phasic weapon (1 CR per unit)
.TP
.BR S= N
Shield defense (1 CR per unit)
.TP
.BR L= N
Torpedo launchers (1 CR per launcher)
.TP
.BR T= N
Torpedoes (1 CR per 3 torpedoes)
.TP
.BR H= N
SystemShip hangars (1 CR per hangar, WarpShips only)
.PP
\fBExample:\fR BS FALCON PD=7 P=3 S=2 L=1 T=6
.RE
.TP
.B build commit \fItarget\fR\fR, \fBBC \fItarget\fR
Finalize a draft and add the ship to your fleet.
CR is deducted.
The ship appears in the fleet but must be deployed before it can act.
.TP
.B build cancel \fItarget\fR\fR, \fBbx \fItarget\fR
Cancel a draft.
No CR is deducted.
.SS "Deployment Commands"
.TP
.B deploy \fIship\fR \fIsystem\fR\fR, \fBds \fIship\fR \fIsystem\fR
Deploy a newly built ship to a base star system.
.RS
.PP
The first deployment by either player claims a side of the map.
The three base stars on that side become your home bases.
Subsequent deployments must go to one of your claimed base stars.
.RE
.SS "Movement Commands"
.TP
.B move \fIship\fR \fIdest\fR [\fIvia...\fR]\fR, \fBm \fIship\fR \fIdest\fR [\fIvia...\fR]
Move a WarpShip to \fIdest\fR, optionally through intermediate waypoints.
Waypoints may be star system names or hex IDs (e.g., h1310).
Movement costs 1 PD per hex or per warpline jump.
.RS
.PP
\fBCritical rule:\fR WarpShips are forced to stop at any star hex occupied
by enemy ships.
Combat is triggered at the end of the movement phase.
.PP
PD expended for movement is \fInot\fR permanently reduced.
Full PD is available for combat power allocation.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR M W6 TAVON XYLEN
.br
W6 moves to Tavon, then onward to Xylen.
.RE
.TP
.B pick \fIsystemship\fR \fIwarpship\fR\fR, \fBpu \fIsystemship\fR \fIwarpship\fR
Load a SystemShip onto a WarpShip's rack.
Costs 1 PD during the movement phase.
Ships must be at the same hex.
.TP
.B drop \fIsystemship\fR \fIwarpship\fR\fR, \fBdo \fIsystemship\fR \fIwarpship\fR
Unload a SystemShip from a WarpShip.
Costs 1 PD during the movement phase.
.SS "Combat Commands"
.TP
.B combat\fR, \fBcm
Show active combat status at all contested hexes.
.TP
.B combat order \fIship\fR \fItactic\fR \fItarget\fR \fIallocation\fR\fR, \fBco ...
Issue a combat order for one of your ships.
.RS
.PP
\fBTactics:\fR
.TP
.B a\fR (attack)
Aggressive offense.
Best at 0 to +2 drive advantage.
.TP
.B d\fR (dodge)
Defensive maneuver.
Good at +1/+2 drive, hard to hit at 0/\-1.
.TP
.B e\fR (escape/retreat)
Attempt to flee combat.
WarpShips only.
SystemShips may never select escape.
.PP
\fBAllocation:\fR PD=N P=N S=N L=N T=N
.PP
\fBPhasic fire example:\fR
.nf
> CO W3 A S25 PD=2 P=3 S=2
.fi
W3 attacks S25: 2 drive, 3 phasic, 2 shield.
.PP
\fBTorpedo fire example:\fR
.nf
> CO S25 D W3 PD=4 L=1 T=3
.fi
S25 dodges while firing 1 torpedo at W3 with torpedo drive=3.
Each \fBT=\fR parameter specifies that torpedo's drive power.
If two torpedoes were fires, the player would need to use two Launchers:
\fBL=2\fR and then assign \fIeach\fR Torpedo power: \fBT=3\fR \fBT=2\fR for 
power assignments 3 and 2 respectively.
.RE
.TP
.B combat commit\fR, \fBcc
Lock in all pending combat orders.
Once both players commit, the combat round resolves automatically.
.TP
.B combat cancel\fR, \fBcx
Cancel all pending combat orders.
.TP
.B combat drafts\fR, \fBcd
Show your pending (uncommitted) combat orders.
.TP
.B combat apply \fIship\fR \fIallocation\fR\fR, \fBca \fIship\fR \fIallocation\fR
Assign damage to a ship's attributes after combat resolution.
.RS
.PP
\fBExample:\fR CA W3 PD=2 P=1 S=2
.br
Distributes 5 total damage: 2 to PD, 1 to Phasic, 2 to Shield.
The total assigned must equal the damage dealt (or consume all
remaining HP if the ship is destroyed).
.RE
.TP
.B retreat \fIship\fR \fIhex\fR\fR, \fBrt \fIship\fR \fIhex\fR
Retreat a ship from combat to an adjacent hex.
Retreat destinations are validated against the hex adjacency graph.
.SS "Repair and Resupply Commands"
.TP
.B repair \fIship\fR \fIattr\fR=\fIN\fR\fR, \fBrp \fIship\fR \fIattr\fR=\fIN\fR
Repair a damaged ship.
1 CR per attribute point restored.
Ship must start the turn at a friendly base star hex (or at a
REPAIR_DOCK or SHIPYARD facility).
Cannot repair beyond original built capacity.
One attribute per command invocation.
.RS
.PP
\fBExample:\fR rp W3 pd=2
.RE
.TP
.B resupply \fIship\fR \fIquantity\fR\fR, \fBrs \fIship\fR \fIquantity\fR
Resupply torpedoes at 1 CR per 3 torpedoes (up to original capacity).
Ship must be at a friendly base star hex.
.SS "Economy Commands"
.TP
.B extract scan\fR, \fBex scan
Scan the current system for harvestable resources.
Requires a ship with LRS (Long Range Scanner) at the system.
Yield is modified by your knowledge level (see MILIEU CODEX).
.TP
.B extract \fIship\fR \fIresource\fR\fR, \fBex \fIship\fR \fIresource\fR
Extract a resource from the current system into the ship's cargo hold.
Multi-word resources use spaces: \fBex W1 rare earth\fR.
.TP
.B market\fR, \fBmk
Display current market prices with trend indicators
(RISING, STABLE, FALLING).
.TP
.B market \fIresource\fR\fR, \fBmk \fIresource\fR
Show price history for a specific resource.
.TP
.B trade list\fR, \fBtr list
Show base trade prices for all resources.
.TP
.B trade buy \fIresource\fR \fIquantity\fR\fR, \fBtr buy ...
Purchase resources at the current market price, deducting credits.
.TP
.B trade sell \fIresource\fR \fIquantity\fR\fR, \fBtr sell ...
Sell resources from cargo at 75% of market price.
.TP
.B trade transfer \fIfrom\fR \fIto\fR \fIresource\fR \fIqty\fR\fR, \fBtr ts ...
Move resources between ships at the same location.
.TP
.B fabricate list\fR, \fBfab list
Show available fabrication recipes and their material costs.
.TP
.B fabricate \fIrecipe\fR [\fIquantity\fR]\fR, \fBfab \fIrecipe\fR ...
Manufacture items from raw materials in cargo.
.TP
.B outfit \fIship\fR lrs\fR, \fBout \fIship\fR ...
Install equipment on a ship.
.TP
.B outfit list\fR, \fBout list
Show available equipment and their credit costs.
.TP
.B salvage scan\fR, \fBjk scan
Scan for salvageable objects at the current hex.
Discovery chance is modified by knowledge level.
.TP
.B salvage \fIship\fR\fR, \fBjk \fIship\fR
Conduct a salvage operation at the current hex.
Hazard chance is modified by knowledge level.
.SS "Development Commands"
.TP
.B gamedev\fR, \fBgd
Show current development override status.
.TP
.B gamedev \fIsubcmd\fR \fIvalue\fR\fR, \fBgd \fIsubcmd\fR ...
Set development overrides.
Subcommands: env, combat, move, crt.
.TP
.B gamedev reset\fR, \fBgd reset
Clear all development overrides.
.\" ===================================================================
.SH COMBAT RESOLUTION
Combat is the heart of Kepler's Horizon.
It is entirely deterministic: given the same orders, the same results
will always occur.
There are no random rolls.
.SS "Combat Trigger"
Combat is triggered when opposing ships occupy the same star hex at the
end of the movement phase.
The system creates a \fBcombat_state\fR record for that hex and notifies
both players.
.SS "Combat Stages"
Combat at each hex proceeds through four stages in a cycle:
.TS
tab(|);
l l l.
Stage|Name|Description
_
0|ORDERS|Both players issue orders for their ships
1|RESOLVE_READY|System resolves the round (automatic)
2|DAMAGE_PENDING|Players assign damage to their ships
3|RETREAT_PENDING|Stalemate: initiative player must withdraw
.TE
.SS "The Combat Results Table (CRT)"
The CRT is the core lookup table that determines whether a weapon
strikes its target.
It is indexed by three values:
.PP
.RS
1. Firing ship's tactic (Attack, Dodge, or Retreat)
.br
2. Target ship's tactic
.br
3. Drive Difference = Firing Drive \- Target Drive
.RE
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
l l l l l.
Firing|Drive Diff|vs Attack|vs Dodge|vs Retreat
_
Attack|\-3 or less|Miss|Miss|Escapes
Attack|\-1, \-2|Hit|Miss|Escapes
Attack|0, +1|Hit+2|Miss|Miss
Attack|+2|Hit+1|Hit+1|Miss
Attack|+3, +4|Miss|Hit|Hit
Attack|+5+|Miss|Miss|Miss
_
Dodge|\-4 or less|Miss|Miss|Escapes
Dodge|\-2, \-3|Miss|Hit|Escapes
Dodge|0, \-1|Hit|Hit|Escapes
Dodge|+1, +2|Hit|Miss|Escapes
Dodge|+3+|Miss|Miss|Escapes
_
Retreat|\-2 or less|Miss|Miss|Escapes
Retreat|\-1, 0|Hit|Miss|Escapes
Retreat|+1+|Miss|Miss|Escapes
.TE
.PP
Observe:
.IP \(bu 2
Attack vs Attack at 0/+1 drive difference yields Hit+2\(emthe deadliest
intersection.
Aggressive players who match drives annihilate each other.
.IP \(bu 2
A drive difference of +5 or greater is always a miss\(emthe attacker
overshoots.
This prevents dump-stat strategies.
.IP \(bu 2
Dodge is safest at 0/\-1 vs Attack (miss), but vulnerable at
0/\-1 vs Dodge (hit).
.IP \(bu 2
Retreat succeeds ("Escapes") against any tactic at favorable drive
differentials, but fails if the enemy's drive advantage is too high.
.SS "Power Allocation"
Each combat round, a player allocates their ship's PD to subsystems:
.TP
.B Drive (D)
Maneuverability.
Determines the row/column on the CRT.
Higher drive = better chance to hit or dodge, but diverts power
from weapons and shields.
.TP
.B Phasic (B)
Weapon power.
Cannot exceed the ship's built phasic rating.
Damage on hit = phasic allocation + tech level.
.TP
.B Shield (S)
Defensive shield.
Cannot exceed the ship's built shield rating.
Absorption = shield allocation + tech level.
.TP
.B Launchers (T)
Number of torpedoes to fire.
Each launcher requires 1 PD.
.PP
\fBConstraint:\fR D + P + S + L \[<=] current PD.
.PP
\fBCritical rule:\fR Phasics/Shields and Torpedoes cannot be used in the
same round.
If you fire torpedoes, you cannot power phasics or shields.
.SS "Phasic Damage"
When a phasic attack hits:
.IP 1. 3
Damage = Phasic power allocation + Tech level
.IP 2. 3
If target powered shields: absorption = Shield allocation + Tech level
.IP 3. 3
Net damage = max(0, damage \- absorption)
.SS "Torpedo Damage"
Each torpedo is individually resolved:
.IP \(bu 2
Torpedoes always use the \fBAttack\fR tactic on the CRT
.IP \(bu 2
Each torpedo has its own drive setting (specified via \fBT=N\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
Damage per hitting torpedo = 2 + Tech level
.IP \(bu 2
Torpedoes are expended on firing (regardless of hit/miss)
.IP \(bu 2
Each launcher fires at most 1 torpedo per round
.SS "Damage Assignment"
After resolution (Stage 2), each player must assign their ship's
damage to specific attributes using \fBcombat apply\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Total assigned must equal damage dealt
.IP \(bu 2
If damage exceeds remaining HP, the ship is destroyed (overkill accepted)
.IP \(bu 2
Ship is destroyed when PD + P + S + L + T + H = 0
.IP \(bu 2
The Warp Generator is never damaged
.SS "Retreat"
A ship using the Escape tactic must receive "Escapes" results against
\fIall\fR enemy ships firing at it.
If any attacker rolls a Hit, the retreat fails and the ship remains
in combat.
.PP
On successful retreat, the ship is flagged \fBescape_pending\fR.
The player must issue \fBretreat \fIship\fR \fIhex\fR\fR to move the
ship to an adjacent hex.
The turn is blocked until all retreats are completed.
.PP
SystemShips may never retreat.
They must be carried out by a WarpShip using the Pick/Drop mechanism.
.SS "Stalemate"
If three consecutive combat rounds produce zero unabsorbed damage
(stalemate_counter >= 3), the initiative player (who moved into the hex)
must withdraw all their ships from the hex.
The defender retains control.
.SS "Combat End Conditions"
Combat at a hex ends when:
.IP \(bu 2
All ships of one side are destroyed or retreated
.IP \(bu 2
Stalemate forces the initiative player to withdraw
.\" ===================================================================
.SH MOVEMENT RULES
.IP 1. 3
WarpShips \fBmust stop\fR at star hexes occupied by enemy ships.
.IP 2. 3
WarpShips may move through space hexes with enemy ships.
.IP 3. 3
Warplines are treated as space hexes for movement purposes.
Entering a warpline at one end and exiting at the other costs 1 PD.
.IP 4. 3
Ships with PD=0 cannot move.
.IP 5. 3
PD used for movement is \fBnot permanently expended\fR.
Full PD is available for combat power allocation.
This is a critical rule: movement does not weaken your ships for battle.
.IP 6. 3
Cannot move onto enemy base star hex during turn 1.
.IP 7. 3
Hex events (NAVIGATION_HAZARD) may increase movement cost through
specific hexes.
.PP
The server uses BFS pathfinding to calculate minimum PD expenditure.
Players may specify intermediate waypoints to force a specific route.
.\" ===================================================================
.SH VICTORY CONDITIONS
.TP
.B Dominance Victory
Accumulate 3 victory points by occupying enemy base stars at the
start of your turn.
Each enemy base star hex with at least one of your ships earns 1 VP.
.TP
.B Elimination Victory
Destroy all enemy ships.
.TP
.B Draw
Neither player has effective ships remaining.
.\" ===================================================================
