.SH AI AGENT
Kepler's Horizon supports single-player mode through an autonomous AI
opponent called the \fBAutonomy Agency\fR (Generation 4).
The AI replaces one human player and executes commands through the same
game engine, respecting all rules and constraints.
It issues the same textual commands a human player would type,
funneled through the same \f(CWyyparse()\fR grammar.
.PP
The AI thread runs independently of HTTP request handling.
When a game ends (via \fBquit\fR, victory, or elimination), the AI's
game context is zeroed\(emit is \fBnever killed\fR.
The thread stays alive and naturally ceases operating when it detects
\fBm_game_id = 0\fR on its next gather cycle.
.SS "Architecture: Mealy State Machine"
The AI operates as a closed-loop Mealy state machine with three phases
per decision cycle:
.IP "1. GATHER (C++)" 4
Populate the \fBAASlate\fR from the database and StateMachine.
The Slate is a complete snapshot of observable game state: fleet
positions and attributes, combat theaters, economic data, territory
control, BFS distance matrix, hex adjacency, warpline topology,
codex knowledge levels, market prices, facility control, salvage
sites, and persisted cross-turn metrics.
Nothing is hidden from the AI that a human player could see.
.IP "2. CALCULATE (Lisp)" 4
Marshal the slate to Common Lisp via ECL (Embeddable Common Lisp)
and call \fB(aa-calculate slate)\fR.
The Lisp layer computes a strategic state once, then dispatches to
a phase-specific decision pipeline that fires named rules, evaluates
a goal graph, and returns a list of command strings plus optional
metric write-backs.
.IP "3. RENDER (C++)" 4
Inject each resulting command into the game engine via
\fBAICommandInjector\fR, exactly as if a human typed it.
The \f(CWTaskRunner\fR thread executes the command through
\f(CWyyparse()\fR and blocks until completion.
The AI observes the result and feeds it back into the next
GATHER cycle, making the loop adaptive: failed commands change
the slate, and CALCULATE derives a different action on the next
iteration.
.PP
The cycle repeats (gather, recalculate, render) until the AI issues
a terminal command (\fBNEXT\fR or \fBDONE\fR) to advance the phase
or end the turn.
Every decision is based on current game state, never stale assumptions.
.PP
A safety guard at the top of \fBgather()\fR checks \fBm_game_id <= 0\fR
and sets \fBgame_over = true\fR, causing the Mealy loop to exit
gracefully without querying the database.
.SS "Generation 4 DSL Architecture"
The Generation 4 AI is a declarative, rule-driven system built on
three macro primitives defined in \fBaa-macros.lisp\fR:
.IP "\fBdefentity\fR" 12
Declares an entity type (ship, combat, resource, facility, etc.)
and auto-generates typed accessor functions.
Entity fields support aliases, defaults, transforms, and derived
computations.
.IP "\fBdefine-strategy-rule\fR" 12
Registers a named decision rule with a phase tag, priority, guard
predicate (\fB:when\fR), and action body (\fB:action\fR).
Rules are dispatched by a rule engine that evaluates guards in
priority order and fires the first match.
.IP "\fBdefgoal\fR" 12
Declares a goal node in a dependency graph rooted at
\fB:win-game\fR.
Each goal carries a priority, a parent link (\fB:supported-by\fR),
satisfaction and precondition predicates, an action function,
and a resource-needs declaration.
The goal graph is validated at load time to ensure all goals
trace back to the root with no cycles.
.PP
All Lisp functions are pure: slate in, decisions out.
No function mutates itself or carries hidden state.
This makes every decision deterministic, debuggable, and auditable.
.SS "Strategy DSL Modules"
The AI strategy is implemented across twelve Common Lisp modules,
loaded in strict dependency order by the C++ runtime:
.TS
tab(|);
l l l.
Order|Module|Purpose
_
1|aa-macros.lisp|DSL infrastructure: defentity, define-strategy-rule, defgoal
2|aa-theta.lisp|Parameter vector: all tunable constants (defparameter)
3|aa-entities.lisp|Entity ontology: ship, combat, resource, facility, etc.
4|aa-util.lisp|Slate accessors, ship finders, predicates, command helpers
5|aa-strategy.lisp|Strategic state computation: posture, theaters, force projection
6|aa-goals.lisp|Goal dependency graph and MPC evaluation engine
7|aa-rules.lisp|Named decision rules across all phases (build, move, combat, etc.)
8|aa-economic.lisp|Economic action generators: survey, extract, trade, fabricate
9|aa-build.lisp|Ship design, construction, repair, deployment
10|aa-movement.lisp|Movement routing, threat detection, target assignment
11|aa-combat.lisp|Combat triage, CRT-aware tactics, damage assignment
12|aa-core.lisp|Entry point and phase dispatcher
.TE
.SS "The Strategic State"
At the start of each CALCULATE cycle, \fBcompute-strategic-state\fR
runs once and produces a strategy plist consumed by all downstream
decisions.
The strategic state comprises:
.IP "\fBVictory State\fR" 4
Bases held, bases needed, score comparison, VP race projection,
endgame detection (either side within one base of winning).
.IP "\fBFleet State\fR" 4
Ship counts by role, total combat attributes, weighted combat power
(PD + 1.5B + 1.2S + 0.5T + 0.3M scaled by health ratio), and fleet
composition analysis.
.IP "\fBEnemy Profile\fR" 4
Enemy ship count, positions, maximum observed tech, torpedo capability,
shield density, and estimated total CR investment.
Cross-turn metrics track historical enemy positions and watermarks
from combat observations.
.IP "\fBHex Valuation\fR" 4
Each hex receives a strategic score: enemy base (100), own base (80),
facility bonuses (shipyard 30, refinery 20, repair 15, trade 10),
proximity to bases, warpline chokepoints (25), and VP-occupied
bonus (40).
.IP "\fBForce Projection\fR" 4
Undefended enemy bases, bases under threat, reinforcement availability,
convergence points (hexes reachable by 2+ friendly ships), and
coverage gaps (own bases outside friendly reach).
.IP "\fBEnemy Intent\fR" 4
Estimated convergence target, threat velocity per base, inferred
spending rate, and strategy classification (aggressive, defensive,
tech-investing, balanced).
.IP "\fBTemporal State\fR" 4
Turns until next tech level, game phase (early/mid/late), credit
trend, power trend, fleet attrition rate, and 3-turn economic
trajectory.
.IP "\fBPosture\fR" 4
Result of firing posture rules (last-match-wins semantics).
One of: aggressive, defensive, or balanced.
Determines acceptable loss thresholds and build/movement directives.
.SS "Phase Dispatch"
The entry point \fB(aa-calculate slate)\fR computes the strategic
state, then dispatches to the current phase:
.TP
.B Build (Phase 0)
The rule engine fires build rules in priority order: commit pending
drafts, set ship specifications from design templates, deploy
completed ships, repair damaged ships at bases, evaluate economic
goals, build defenders for unguarded bases, build new WarpShips.
Falls through to \fBNEXT\fR when no rule produces an action.
.TP
.B Movement (Phase 1)
Checks threatened bases first (enemy within 3 hops) and dispatches
defenders.
Then moves idle ships toward enemy bases, spreading across targets
to maximize VP coverage.
Uses precomputed BFS distances from the slate.
.TP
.B Combat (Phase 2)
Triages all active theaters simultaneously.
Each theater is assessed as fight, hold, or retreat based on force
ratio and hex value.
Ships needing retreat are handled first, then pending damage
assignment, then combat orders, then commit.
.TP
.B Pick/Drop (Phase 3)
Rearranges SystemShips at contested or strategic hexes.
.TP
.B End Turn (Phase 4)
Issues \fBDONE\fR to pass initiative.
.SS "The Goal Graph"
Economic decisions are driven by a directed acyclic graph of goals
rooted at \fB:win-game\fR.
Each goal declares a priority, a satisfaction predicate, a
precondition predicate, and an action function.
The MPC evaluator walks goals in priority order, finds the
highest-priority goal that is both unsatisfied and actionable
(precondition met), and executes its action.
.PP
Goals compose through dependency: a goal's action may require
sub-goals to be satisfied first.
The \fB:needed-resources\fR declaration prevents the sell-excess
logic from liquidating cargo that serves an active goal.
.PP
.ne 7
The current goal graph:
.TS
tab(|);
l l l l.
Goal|Pri|Parent|Trigger
_
:maintain-torpedo-stock|1|:win-game|Fleet torpedoes below 50%
:resupply-torpedoes|2|:maintain-torpedo-stock|Ship on base with depleted torpedoes
:fabricate-materiel|3|:maintain-torpedo-stock|Ship at shipyard with ingredients
:acquire-resource|4|:fabricate-materiel|Missing fabrication ingredient
:survey-for-yield|5|:acquire-resource|System knowledge below Surveyed
:salvage-opportunity|6|:win-game|Ship at hex with salvageable wreckage
:sell-excess-cargo|8|:win-game|Cargo serving no active goal, above price floor
.TE
.SS "The Rule Engine"
Strategy rules are registered via \fBdefine-strategy-rule\fR with a
phase tag (\fB:build\fR, \fB:move\fR, \fB:combat\fR, \fB:triage\fR,
\fB:tactics\fR, \fB:design\fR, \fB:posture\fR, \fB:pickdrop\fR),
a priority (lower fires first), a guard predicate, and an action body.
.PP
Three dispatch modes:
.IP "\fBfire-first-matching-rule\fR" 4
Evaluates guards in priority order; fires the first that returns true.
Used for combat orders and pick/drop.
.IP "\fBfire-first-producing-rule\fR" 4
Same, but the action must return a non-nil result.
Used for build and movement where a rule may match but produce no
command (e.g., build rule matches but player cannot afford the ship).
.IP "\fBfire-posture-rules\fR" 4
Evaluates all rules; last match wins.
Used exclusively for posture computation where later rules
override earlier ones.
.SS "Build Strategy"
The AI selects from predefined ship templates defined in
\fBaa-theta.lisp\fR:
.TS
tab(|);
l l l.
Template|Attributes|Role
_
Brawler|PD=6 P=4 S=3|Main battle ship; default first build
Interceptor|PD=5 P=2 S=2|Fast pursuit; spreads across enemy bases
Torpedo Boat|PD=4 S=1 L=2 T=6|Alpha strike; counters heavy shields
Fortress|PD=8 P=6 S=5|SystemShip for base defense
Defender|PD=6 P=4 S=3|SystemShip garrison
.TE
.PP
Design selection is driven by design rules: the first build is always
a Brawler; torpedo boats are chosen when the enemy fields heavy
shields; interceptors fill the fleet for base coverage; defenders
garrison unguarded home bases.
.PP
Build heuristics gate construction on CR availability, tech-level
timing (the AI may delay a build if tech upgrades within 1\-2 turns),
and fleet size relative to the enemy.
.SS "Combat Strategy"
Combat operates at two levels: theater triage and per-ship tactics.
.PP
\fBTheater Triage.\fR
All active combats are assessed simultaneously before any orders
are issued.
Each theater receives a verdict (fight, hold, or retreat) based on
force ratio, hex strategic value, and stalemate count.
VP hexes are fought more aggressively; non-VP hexes with
unfavorable force ratios are abandoned.
.PP
\fBTactics.\fR
Per-ship orders are generated by tactics rules:
.IP \(bu 2
Focus fire: all ships in a theater target the same enemy
(lowest total HP).
.IP \(bu 2
CRT-aware power allocation caps drive differential at +4 to avoid
the +5 automatic miss.
Three allocation modes: standard attack (target diff +2), pursuit
(+4), and counter-dodge (+3).
.IP \(bu 2
Torpedo boats fire when they have stock; retreat when empty.
.IP \(bu 2
Stalemate breaker: if the AI is the attacker and stalemate count
reaches 2, it forces maximum damage output or retreats.
.IP \(bu 2
Damage assignment sacrifices attributes in order:
launchers, phasics, shields, PD (preserved last for retreat capability).
.SS "Economic Strategy"
Economic actions are driven by the goal graph.
The MPC evaluator selects the highest-priority unsatisfied goal
and dispatches its action generator.
Action generators map goals to concrete commands:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBResupply:\fR reload torpedoes at own base.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFabricate:\fR convert raw materials to torpedoes at a shipyard
(recipe: 2 Fe + 1 Rd + 1 Vo).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAcquire:\fR extract resources from a deposit or buy them at
a trade hub.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSurvey:\fR improve system knowledge to boost extraction yield.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSalvage:\fR recover materials from wreckage.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSell:\fR liquidate excess cargo that serves no active goal,
only if the market price exceeds 80% of base value.
.SS "The Theta Parameter Vector"
All tunable numeric constants (thresholds, weights, fleet caps,
template attributes) are declared as \fBdefparameter\fR values in
\fBaa-theta.lisp\fR and accessed via \fB(theta 'theta-xxx)\fR.
An unknown key raises an error at load time, preventing silent
misconfiguration.
This design enables build-time parameter tuning from game telemetry
without modifying strategy code.
.SS "Cross-Turn Memory"
The AI persists deduced metrics to the \fBaa_metrics\fR database table
via \fB(make-metric name value)\fR pseudo-commands emitted from Lisp.
These metrics survive across decision cycles and are loaded back into
the slate each GATHER.
.PP
Metric categories:
.IP \(bu 2
Trend snapshots (credits, fleet power, economic trajectory)
.IP \(bu 2
Enemy position history (per-ship hex snapshots)
.IP \(bu 2
Enemy distance-to-base (closing rate inference)
.IP \(bu 2
Combat watermarks (maximum observed PD, phasic, shield, launchers per enemy)
.IP \(bu 2
Rack inference (enemy ship carried SystemShips)
.IP \(bu 2
Per-theater engagement history (rounds fought, power committed)
.IP \(bu 2
Enemy economic estimate (inferred total CR investment)
.\" ===================================================================
.SH SYSTEMSHIP PICKUP AND DROP
.SS "During Movement Phase"
.IP \(bu 2
Costs 1 PD per SystemShip picked up or dropped
.IP \(bu 2
Can be performed at any star hex during movement
.SS "During Combat"
.IP \(bu 2
Requires: Drive=0, Shield=0, Dodge or Escape tactic
.IP \(bu 2
Only 1 SystemShip per combat round
.IP \(bu 2
May fire Phasic (but not Torpedoes) while picking up/dropping
.IP \(bu 2
Dropped SystemShips cannot fire that round
.IP \(bu 2
Picked up SystemShips cannot fire but may power Shields
.SS "After Combat (Phase 5)"
.IP \(bu 2
Free rearrangement of SystemShips at star hexes
.IP \(bu 2
No PD cost
.IP \(bu 2
Active player only
.\" ===================================================================
.SH BUILD POINT COSTS
.TS
tab(|);
l l.
Attribute|Cost
_
Power/Drive (PD)|1 CR per unit
Warp Generator (WG)|5 CR (WarpShips only, automatic)
Phasic (P)|1 CR per unit
Shield (S)|1 CR per unit
Launcher (L)|1 CR per launcher
Torpedoes (T)|1 CR per 3 torpedoes
SystemShip Hangar (H)|1 CR per hangar (WarpShips only)
.TE
.\" ===================================================================
.SH CONSTRAINTS AND INVARIANTS
These are the fundamental laws of the game engine.
They are never violated, and no command or sequence of commands can
circumvent them:
.IP \(bu 2
No randomness in combat\(emall outcomes are deterministic.
.IP \(bu 2
No hidden information\(emall game state is public.
.IP \(bu 2
Rules do not change mid-game.
.IP \(bu 2
Equal inputs yield equal outcomes.
.IP \(bu 2
Ships retain original tech level permanently.
.IP \(bu 2
Warp Generators never take damage.
A ship is destroyed when all attributes except WG reach 0.
.IP \(bu 2
SystemShips cannot have Warp Generators or SystemShip Racks.
.IP \(bu 2
WarpShips cannot be carried in SystemShip Racks.
.IP \(bu 2
Phasics/Shields and Torpedoes cannot be used in the same combat round.
.IP \(bu 2
SystemShips may never select the Escape tactic.
.IP \(bu 2
PD used for movement is not permanently expended.
.IP \(bu 2
Repairs cannot exceed original built capacity.
.IP \(bu 2
Torpedo resupply cannot exceed original torpedo capacity.
.\" ===================================================================
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS "Building a WarpShip"
.nf
> \fBbn\fR W FALCON
> \fBbs\fR FALCON PD=5 P=3 S=2 L=1 T=3
> \fBbc\fR FALCON
.fi
.PP
Total cost: PD(5) + P(3) + S(2) + L(1) + T(1) + WG(5) = 17 CR.
Tech level determined by current round.
.SS "Building a SystemShip"
.nf
> \fBBN\fR S AVENGER
> \fBBS\fR AVENGER PD=1 L=1 T=6
> \fBBC\fR AVENGER
.fi
.PP
Total cost: PD(1) + L(1) + T(2) = 4 CR.
No Warp Generator required.
.SS "Movement"
.nf
> \fBM\fR W6 Tavon Xylen
.fi
.PP
W6 moves from current hex to Tavon via the nearest warpline path,
then onward to Xylen.
Total PD cost depends on path length (BFS-computed).
.SS "Combat Sequence"
.nf
> \fBCO\fR W3 A S25 PD=2 P=3 S=2
> \fBCC\fR
\fI(Wait for opponent to commit...)\fR
\fI(Resolution: W3 hits S25 for 3+tech damage)\fR
> \fBCA\fR S25 PD=2 P=1
.fi
.SS "Torpedo Fire"
.nf
> \fBCO\fR S25 D W3 PD=4 L=1 T=3
> \fBCC\fR
.fi
.PP
S25 dodges while firing 1 torpedo at W3.
The torpedo uses Attack tactic with drive=3 on the Torpedo.
Torpedo damage on hit = 2 + tech level.
.SS "Economic Workflow"
.nf
> \fBout\fR W1 lrs
> \fBsurvey\fR ARVEN
> \fBextract scan\fR
> \fBex\fR W1 ferrous
> \fBmarket\fR
> \fBtrade sell\fR ferrous 5
> \fBfab\fR torpedoes W1
.fi
.SS "Save and Load"
.nf
> \fBsave\fR my-game
> \fBload\fR my-game
\fI(Other player must type:)\fR
> \fBaccept\fR my-game
.fi
.SS "Quit"
.nf
> \fBquit\fR
\fIQUIT: Game auto-saved as 'autosaved-2026-02-13-14-30'.\fR
\fIReturning to lobby.\fR
.fi
The game is auto-saved.
Combat state is cleared.
The other player is notified and redirected.
The AI thread ceases operating on this game.
.\" ===================================================================
