.SH "ADDING A GOAL TO THE AI AGENT"
This section documents the procedure for adding a new goal to the
Generation 4 AI Agent's goal graph.
A goal is a declarative objective that the MPC evaluator can select,
prioritize, and execute during the build phase of the AI's turn.
.PP
The example used throughout is a hypothetical goal called
\fB:fortify-chokepoint\fR, whose purpose is to deploy a SystemShip
defender at a warpline chokepoint hex when the AI controls that hex
and it is ungarrisoned.
.SS "Step 1: Define the Goal (aa-goals.lisp)"
Every goal is declared via the \fBdefgoal\fR macro.
The declaration establishes the goal's identity, its position in
the dependency graph, and the predicates and action that govern it.
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ft CW
(defgoal :fortify-chokepoint
  :priority 7
  :supported-by :win-game
  :satisfied
    (null (find-unfortified-chokepoints slate))
  :precondition
    (and (>= (slate-credits slate) 200)
         (not (null (find-unfortified-chokepoints slate))))
  :action
    (issue-fortify-chokepoint slate goals)
  :needed-resources nil
  :doc "Build and deploy a defender at an ungarrisoned chokepoint.")
.ft R
.fi
.RE
.PP
The fields:
.TP
.B :priority
Integer.
Lower numbers are higher priority.
The evaluator walks goals in ascending priority order and selects
the first that is unsatisfied and actionable.
Choose a value that places the goal correctly relative to existing
goals (see the goal table in the AI AGENT section).
.TP
.B :supported-by
Keyword naming the parent goal.
Every goal must trace to \fB:win-game\fR through its parent chain.
The graph is validated at load time; a dangling parent is a fatal
error.
.TP
.B :satisfied
A form evaluated with the current slate bound.
Returns true when the goal is already met and needs no action.
The evaluator skips satisfied goals.
.TP
.B :precondition
A form evaluated with the current slate bound.
Returns true when the goal is actionable (i.e., the game state
permits the goal's action to succeed).
An unsatisfied goal with a false precondition is skipped; the
evaluator moves to the next priority.
.TP
.B :action
A form evaluated with both \fBslate\fR and \fBgoals\fR bound.
Must return a list of command plists (via \fBmake-cmd\fR) or
\fBnil\fR.
Returning nil means the goal matched but could not produce a
concrete command this cycle; the evaluator falls through.
.TP
.B :needed-resources
A form returning a list of resource-type strings (e.g.,
\fB'("FERROUS" "VOLATILE")\fR) that this goal's fulfillment
consumes.
The sell-excess logic checks this list before liquidating cargo.
Use \fBnil\fR if the goal consumes no cargo.
.SS "Step 2: Implement the Finder (aa-util.lisp or aa-goals.lisp)"
The satisfaction and precondition predicates typically call a finder
function that locates the game-state entity the goal acts upon.
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ft CW
(defun find-unfortified-chokepoints (slate)
  "Return list of chokepoint hexes we control but have not garrisoned.
   A chokepoint is a hex with 3+ warpline connections bridging
   own-territory and enemy-territory."
  (let ((result nil))
    (dolist (hex (slate-chokepoint-hexes slate))
      (let ((garrison (count-if
                        (lambda (s)
                          (and (not (ship-warpship s))
                               (string= (ship-hex s) hex)))
                        (slate-own-ships slate))))
        (when (zerop garrison)
          (push hex result))))
    result))
.ft R
.fi
.RE
.PP
Conventions for finder functions:
.IP \(bu 2
Return the entity (ship, hex, system) or \fBnil\fR.
For set-valued queries, return a list (empty list counts as nil
for satisfaction tests).
.IP \(bu 2
Accept only the slate as argument.
Side-effect-free.
.IP \(bu 2
Name the function \fBfind-\fIwhat\fB-for-\fIgoal\fR or similar.
.SS "Step 3: Implement the Action Generator"
The action function maps the goal to one or more concrete game
commands.
It returns a list of command plists or nil.
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ft CW
(defun issue-fortify-chokepoint (slate goals)
  "Build a defender SystemShip and deploy to the highest-value
   unfortified chokepoint.  Returns command list or nil."
  (declare (ignore goals))
  (let ((targets (find-unfortified-chokepoints slate)))
    (when targets
      (let ((best (first (sort targets #'>
                          :key (lambda (h)
                                 (compute-hex-value slate h))))))
        (format t "[LISP] fortify-chokepoint -> ~A~%" best)
        (list (make-cmd "BN" "S SENTINEL")
              (make-cmd "BS" "SENTINEL PD=6 P=4 S=3")
              (make-cmd "BC" "SENTINEL")
              (make-cmd "DS"
                (format nil "SENTINEL ~A" best)))))))
.ft R
.fi
.RE
.PP
Conventions for action generators:
.IP \(bu 2
Return \fB(list (make-cmd ...))\fR for success,
\fBnil\fR for "cannot act this cycle."
.IP \(bu 2
Use \fB(make-cmd "CMD" "ARGS")\fR to construct command plists.
The C++ RENDER phase injects these as textual commands through
\f(CWyyparse()\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Log the action via \fBformat\fR to \fBt\fR for diagnostics.
.IP \(bu 2
Multi-command sequences (build + set + commit + deploy) are
legal; the RENDER phase executes them in order.
.SS "Step 4: Add Theta Parameters (aa-theta.lisp)"
If the goal uses any tunable constants, declare them in the theta
parameter vector rather than hardcoding them in the action function.
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ft CW
(defparameter *theta-fortify-min-warplines* 3
  "Minimum warpline connections for a hex to qualify as chokepoint.")

(defparameter *theta-fortify-max-defenders* 1
  "Maximum defender SystemShips to deploy per chokepoint.")
.ft R
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reference them via \fB(theta 'theta-fortify-min-warplines)\fR in
finder and action functions.
.SS "Step 5: Integrate with the Rule Engine (Optional)"
Goals that operate during the build phase are automatically invoked
by the existing economic-dispatch rule in \fBaa-rules.lisp\fR.
No additional rule is needed unless the goal requires action during
a different phase (movement, combat, pick/drop).
.PP
For non-build-phase goals, register a strategy rule:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ft CW
(define-strategy-rule fortify-move-defender
  :phase :move
  :priority 250
  :when (and (find-unfortified-chokepoints slate)
             (find-idle-defender slate))
  :action (issue-move-defender-to-chokepoint slate strategy)
  :doc "Move idle defender toward unfortified chokepoint.")
.ft R
.fi
.RE
.SS "Step 6: Validate the Goal Graph"
The graph validator runs at load time.
It verifies:
.IP \(bu 2
Every goal traces to \fB:win-game\fR via \fB:supported-by\fR links.
.IP \(bu 2
No cycles exist in the dependency chain.
.IP \(bu 2
No duplicate \fB:objective\fR keywords.
.PP
A goal with a dangling parent (\fB:supported-by\fR naming a
nonexistent goal) is a fatal load error.
Test by restarting the server with the AI DSL path and checking
the log for validation output.
.SS "Step 7: Metric Persistence (Optional)"
If the goal benefits from cross-turn memory, emit metrics via
\fBmake-metric\fR in \fBcompute-metrics-to-persist\fR
(\fBaa-core.lisp\fR).
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ft CW
(make-metric "fortify-last-target" best-hex)
(make-metric "fortify-deployed-count" n)
.ft R
.fi
.RE
.PP
These are intercepted by the C++ layer as pseudo-commands,
written to the \fBaa_metrics\fR table, and reappear in the
slate's \fB:metrics\fR alist on the next GATHER.
.SS "Summary: Files Modified"
.TS
tab(|);
l l.
File|Change
_
aa-goals.lisp|defgoal declaration
aa-util.lisp or aa-goals.lisp|Finder function
aa-economic.lisp or new module|Action generator
aa-theta.lisp|Tunable parameters (if any)
aa-rules.lisp|Strategy rule (if non-build phase)
aa-core.lisp|Metric persistence (if cross-turn memory needed)
.TE
.PP
No C++ changes are required.
The C++ GATHER/RENDER loop and the \fBdefgoal\fR macro handle
registration and dispatch automatically.
.\" ===================================================================
.SH "AI AGENT DESIGN NOTES"
.PP
This section documents design considerations and future directions
for the Autonomy Agency.
.SS "Closed-Loop Command Execution"
The Mealy state machine is a closed loop: GATHER reads the world
state after every RENDER, so a failed command changes the slate
(or leaves it unchanged, which itself is information) and CALCULATE
derives a different action on the next cycle.
.PP
For the loop to close tightly, the agent must distinguish between
"the command succeeded and the world changed" and "the command
failed and the world did not change."
The TaskRunner carries both the result code and the error message
from \f(CWinvoke()\fR.
When this information is conveyed back through the slate,
the Lisp code can reason about failures as structured ontology
facts rather than opaque strings:
.IP \(bu 2
\f(CW:precondition-violated :credits-below-threshold\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\f(CW:precondition-violated :hex-in-combat\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\f(CW:precondition-violated :phase-mismatch\fR
.PP
Failure reasons scoped to the ontology dictionary guarantee that
the Lisp code can handle every failure cause, because the set is
finite and known at compile time.
Failures become structured inputs to CALCULATE, not a special
side channel.
.PP
Failure lifetime is phase-scoped: the failure list clears at each
phase transition.
Within a single phase, preconditions are stable (the world changes
only through the agent's own actions).
Combat is the exception, where state changes within a phase as
orders resolve; combat failures may need round-scoped or
stage-scoped clearing.
.SS "Latent Learning: The Diary Concept"
The learning feedback loop separates into two timescales:
.IP "\fBRuntime\fR" 4
Static Lisp, fixed ontology, metrics memory.
No self-modification.
Deterministic and debuggable.
.IP "\fBBuild-time\fR" 4
Ingest accumulated game diaries, produce updated Lisp parameters.
The agent evolves through a controlled, auditable build step.
.PP
The diary extends the \fBaa_metrics\fR table to capture game-over
summaries: ending round, winner, fleet composition at key moments,
strategies attempted and when.
Three translation approaches, ordered by complexity:
.IP "1." 4
\fBParameter Tables.\fR
The diary feeds a statistical pass that emits a
\f(CWdefparameter\fR config file.
Example: "average earliest viable torpedo build = round 5.2
across 47 games" becomes a theta value.
No generated Lisp code.
Simplest and likely captures 80% of the benefit.
.IP "2." 4
\fBRule Selection.\fR
Multiple strategy variants are written by hand (aggressive,
turtle, torpedo-rush).
Diary analysis determines which variants performed best per
opponent profile.
The build step emits a selection table.
.IP "3." 4
\fBLisp Code Generation.\fR
An external tool reads the diary, identifies patterns, and emits
new Lisp functions.
Requires validation against the ontology.
This is a research direction more than an engineering task.
.PP
For approaches 1 and 2: a pre-build script queries the metrics
database, runs the analysis, and writes a \fB.lisp\fR file of
\f(CWdefparameter\fR declarations.
The normal build includes that file.
.PP
The power of the current architecture is that all Lisp functions
are pure: slate in, decisions out.
They carry no hidden state and do not mutate themselves.
This makes them debuggable, predictable, and auditable.
The diary/build-time approach preserves these properties by
restricting evolution to a versioned build step.

